Please critique whether the following solution to the question is correct.

Question:
The bisulfate (or hydrogen sulfate) anion, {eq}HSO_{4}^{-} {/eq}, behaves as a weak acid. The acid ionization equilibrium constant, {eq}K_{a} {/eq}, for {eq}HSO_{4}^{-}(aq) \rightleftharpoons H^{+}(aq) + SO_{4}^{2-}(aq) {/eq} is {eq}1.2 \times 10^{-2} {/eq} at {eq}25.0^{\circ}C {/eq}. Calculate:

(a) The standard Gibbs free energy change, {eq}\Delta G^{\circ} {/eq}, for this reaction.
(b) The equilibrium molalities of a 0.010-molal solution of sodium hydrogen sulfate, {eq}NaHSO_{4} {/eq}, assuming activity coefficients are approximately unity at low concentrations.

Solution:
(a) Using the relationship between the equilibrium constant, {eq}K_{a} {/eq}, and the standard Gibbs free energy change, {eq}\Delta G^{\circ} {/eq}, at constant temperature:
{eq}{\rm{\Delta }}G^{\circ} = - RT\ln K_{a} {/eq}
where {eq}R {/eq} is the gas constant, {eq}T {/eq} is the temperature in Kelvin, and {eq}K_{a} {/eq} is the acid ionization constant. At {eq}25.0^{\circ}C {/eq}, the temperature is {eq}298.15\,K {/eq}. Thus,

{eq}\begin{align*} \Delta G^{\circ} &= - (8.314\, \text{J/K/mol}) \times 298.15\,K \times \ln (1.2 \times 10^{-2})\\ &= - (8.314 \times 298.15) \times \ln (1.2 \times 10^{-2})\\ &= - 3.9\, \text{kJ/mol} \end{align*} {/eq}

Hence, the standard Gibbs free energy change for the reaction is {eq}{\bf{-3.9}}\, \textbf{kJ/mol} {/eq}.

(b) Sodium hydrogen sulfate, {eq}NaHSO_{4} {/eq}, dissociates completely in water:
{eq}NaHSO_{4}(aq) \rightleftharpoons Na^{+}(aq) + HSO_{4}^{-}(aq) {/eq}

In a 0.010-molal {eq}NaHSO_{4} {/eq} solution, the initial molality of {eq}HSO_{4}^{-} {/eq} ions is also 0.010 mol/kg. Using the ionization equilibrium constant, we can find the equilibrium molality of {eq}H^{+} {/eq} ions:
{eq}K_{a} = \frac{\left[H^{+}\right]\left[SO_{4}^{2-}\right]}{\left[HSO_{4}^{-}\right]} = 1.2 \times 10^{-2} {/eq}

{eq}\begin{align*} \left[H^{+}\right] &= \sqrt{K_{a} \times \left[HSO_{4}^{-}\right]}\\ &= \sqrt{(1.2 \times 10^{-2}) \times (0.010\, \text{mol/kg})}\\ &= 0.034\, \text{mol/kg} \end{align*} {/eq}

Since activity coefficients are approximately 1, the molality of {eq}H^{+} {/eq} ions is equal to its concentration. The total amount of {eq}HSO_{4}^{-} {/eq} ions remains constant during the ionization:

{eq}\left[H^{+}\right] + \left[HSO_{4}^{-}\right] = 0.010\, \text{mol/kg} {/eq}

{eq}\left[HSO_{4}^{-}\right] = 0.010\, \text{mol/kg} - 0.034\, \text{mol/kg} = -0.024\, \text{mol/kg} {/eq}

The negative sign indicates the ionization process. Now, we can find the equilibrium molality of {eq}SO_{4}^{2-} {/eq} ions using the ionization constant again:

{eq}\begin{align*} \left[SO_{4}^{2-}\right] &= K_{a} \times \frac{\left[HSO_{4}^{-}\right]}{\left[H^{+}\right]}\\ &= (1.2 \times 10^{-2}) \times \frac{(0.024\, \text{mol/kg})}{(0.034\, \text{mol/kg})}\\ &= 0.20\, \text{mol/kg} \end{align*} {/eq}

Hence, the equilibrium molalities are:
- The equilibrium molality of {eq}H^{+} {/eq} ions is {eq}{\bf{0.034}}\, \textbf{mol/kg} {/eq}.
- The equilibrium molality of {eq}SO_{4}^{2-} {/eq} ions is {eq}{\bf{0.20}}\, \textbf{mol/kg} {/eq}.

