import java.util.Stack;

class Node {
    char val;
    Node left, right;

    Node(char val) {
        this.val = val;
        left = right = null;
    }
}

class Solution {
    Node constructTree(char[] postfix) {
        Stack<Node> stack = new Stack<>();
        for (char c : postfix) {
            if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
                stack.push(new Node(c));
            } else {
                Node right = stack.pop();
                Node left = stack.pop();
                Node node = new Node(c);
                node.left = left;
                node.right = right;
                stack.push(node);
            }
        }
        return stack.pop();
    }

    void printInfix(Node root) {
        if (root == null) return;
        if (Character.isLetter(root.val)) {
            System.out.print(root.val);
            return;
        }
        System.out.print("(");
        printInfix(root.left);
        System.out.print(root.val);
        printInfix(root.right);
        System.out.print(")");
    }
}
```

The `constructTree` method converts a postfix expression into an expression tree. It iterates through the postfix array, creating nodes for operands (letters) and operators. Operators pop two nodes, create a new node with the operator as the value and the popped nodes as children, and push the new node back onto the stack. Finally, the root of the tree is returned. The `printInfix` function performs an inorder traversal of the constructed tree to generate the infix expression.

